			"Message": "",
			"SHA": "ed37ada6740a1c2f3f1b8f650649aad8b69a3314"
		},
		{
			"Author": "Nikola Hristov",
			"Date": "2026-05-01T01:17:23Z",
			"File": [
				"⠁⠀⠀⠀⠀",
				368,
				5
			],
			"Message": "fix(Output): Revert DisableLazyPaint opt-in gate and remove PartZIndexCSS isolation\n\nThe 9cafc7c opt-in gate for RAF/IntersectionObserver overrides (`__LAND_AGGRESSIVE_LAZY_PAINT__`) regressed editor mount behavior—the editor wouldn't load until the user clicked. The actual culprit for the input-starvation symptom was a Vim extension activating into Normal mode, not the polyfill itself.\n\nAdditionally, remove the `isolation: isolate` rule from `.monaco-workbench .part` in PartZIndexCSS. Under WKWebView this rule blocks input event delivery to Monaco's `<textarea class=\"inputarea\">`—the textarea is focusable, cursor blinks, keys press, but nothing reaches the editor's `keydown` listener. The explicit per-part `z-index` values already establish the desired stacking order without the isolation cost.\n\nRestores editor mount behavior while keeping the CSS lazy-paint fix that solves the original \"panels invisible until hover/scroll\" WKWebView bug.",
			"SHA": "2afe4b4615dc1698fd2224b27ab12185c9630df1"
		},
		{
			"Author": "Nikola Hristov",
			"Date": "2026-05-01T00:35:49Z",
			"File": [
				"⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀",
				368,
				3
			],
			"Message": "refactor(Output): Make DisableLazyPaint RAF/IO overrides opt-in\n\nWrap the requestAnimationFrame and IntersectionObserver overrides in a conditional guard. These overrides redirect Monaco's rAF callbacks to the macrotask queue and replace native IntersectionObserver with a fire-on-observe stub that calls getBoundingClientRect() synchronously.\n\nThe problem: both bypass WKWebView's compositor-frame coordination—which also delivers keyboard input. Once Monaco starts rendering (theme load, decorations, cursor blink, minimap, scrollbars), the macrotask queue floods and keystrokes starve, causing the input lag symptom this polyfill was meant to solve.\n\nSolution: make these overrides opt-in via `__LAND_AGGRESSIVE_LAZY_PAINT__ = true` set BEFORE workbench bootstrap. Keep the CSS lazy-paint kill (content-visibility: visible) always applied—it solves the original \"panels invisible until hover/scroll\" WKWebView bug without affecting input latency.\n\nThis complements the recent IPC logging fix (2588fe4) and EditContext polyfill (44e160c) in addressing WKWebView input starvation.",
			"SHA": "9cafc7cb83c34af5447d5ce1356d24728ebe564d"
		},
		{
			"Author": "Nikola Hristov",
			"Date": "2026-05-01T00:15:48Z",
			"File": [
				"⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀",
				368,
				3
			],
			"Message": "perf(Output): Reduce IPC logging to fix channel saturation causing input lag\n\nThe per-invoke `_DevLogForward` calls on every successful `MountainIPCInvoke` were doubling IPC traffic during extension boot (dozens of file:stat/readFile/extensions:getInstalled calls per second). This saturated Tauri's invoke channel (175-280ms queue depth observed) and starved the WebKit message channel that carries both IPC replies AND keystrokes on macOS.\n\nObserved symptom: user types in editor, nothing visible happens, switches focus, then queued keystrokes flush into the newly-focused element (\"characters appearing automatically in chat input\").\n\nChanges:\n- Remove success-case `tauri-invoke` logging in InvokeMountain (failures still forward)\n- Remove fire-and-forget channel stub logging (logger/status-bar fire dozens/sec)\n- Only log stub-hit for `drift` disposition (command not covered by existing stub)\n\nRust-side `[DEV:IPC] invoke:/done:` lines already carry the same data at ns precision and are filterable via `Trace=ipc`.",
			"SHA": "2588fe4706490748982b01d181db632c39bb3114"
		}
	],
	"TimeStamp": 1780506251790
}